### Theorem 3 Constructive probe — proof sketch

**Hypotheses.** Same as Theorem 2.

**Conclusion.** There exists a componentwise probe \(p\in\mathrm{ext}(H_A)\times\mathrm{ext}(H_B)\) such that
\[
C(p)\ge C(\theta_{\mathrm{sep}})-\delta(\varepsilon),
\]
with \(\delta(\varepsilon)\le\Phi(\varepsilon)\). The probe is constructible from marginal optimizers plus a bounded correction.

**Proof sketch.**  
1. **Marginal optimizers.** Compute marginal optimizers \(v_A^\star\in\mathrm{ext}(\mathrm{proj}_A H)\) maximizing \(g\) on the projection and \(v_B^\star\in\mathrm{ext}(\mathrm{proj}_B H)\) maximizing \(h\). The product \((v_A^\star,v_B^\star)\) is the separable candidate.  
2. **Feasibility correction.** If \((v_A^\star,v_B^\star)\in H\), set \(p=(v_A^\star,v_B^\star)\). If not, use the bounded coupling \(\varepsilon\) to argue there exists a nearby feasible vertex \(p\) within a correction radius \(O(\varepsilon)\) (construct by projecting to the feasible set or by small coordinate adjustments guaranteed by the perturbation regime).  
3. **Objective loss bound.** The objective loss from \(\theta_{\mathrm{sep}}\) to \(p\) is controlled by the same perturbation analysis as in Theorem 2, yielding \(\delta(\varepsilon)\le\Phi(\varepsilon)\). Thus the constructed probe attains the claimed near‑optimality. ∎

