**Conclusion**  
Any global maximizer \(\theta^\star\in H\) satisfies \(\theta^\star\in\mathrm{ext}(H)\).

**Proof**  
1. **Existence.** Continuity of \(C\) and compactness of \(H\) guarantee a global maximizer \(\theta^\star\) by the Weierstrass extreme value theorem.  
2. **One‑coordinate reduction.** Fix all coordinates except coordinate \(i\). Define the slice
   \[
   \phi_i(t)=C(\theta_1,\dots,\theta_{i-1},t,\theta_{i+1},\dots,\theta_n).
   \]
   The contribution from the block containing coordinate \(i\) is monotone in that coordinate, and the coupling term restricted to the line is quasiconvex. The sum of a monotone nondecreasing function and a quasiconvex function on an interval attains its maximum at an endpoint: any interior maximizer would contradict quasiconvexity unless the quasiconvex part is flat on an interval, in which case endpoints still achieve the supremum. Hence \(\phi_i\) attains its maximum at \(t=a_i\) or \(t=b_i\).  
3. **Iterate coordinates.** Apply the previous step for every coordinate \(i=1,\dots,n\). For each coordinate the maximizing value must be an endpoint. Therefore \(\theta^\star\) is an endpoint in every coordinate, i.e., a vertex of \(H\). Thus \(\theta^\star\in\mathrm{ext}(H)\). ∎

